if you find your training boring  you shouldn't be doing it  full stop and i don't just mean because  it's hard to be consistent with  something that you don't enjoy doing i  don't just mean because it's no fun i  mean that you won't progress as quickly  if you aren't engaged with what you're  doing  unfortunately a lot of people think of  working out as being terribly boring by  definition perhaps you're picturing  standing on the spot curling something  or doing a regular squat for the 1000th  time this year i mean yeah that's pretty  meh but training should not be boring  training is the most action most of us  will get in our day-to-day lives it  should be challenging and adrenaline  pumping moreover if your training is  dull and boring then you aren't going to  get the most from it that's because as  andrew huberman often explains attention  is the key to plasticity our brains are  wired to learn from events that we deem  important without attention none of the  important chemicals that are supposed to  stimulate neuroplasticity will be  produced  so just why is your training boring and  what can you do about it  simple we train like an action hero  [Music]  three sets of 10 is 30 repetitions of  the precise same movement doing anything  30 times is pretty boring when training  for cardio or conditioning we need even  longer sets and that can be even more  mind-numbing but here's the thing you  don't need to be doing three sets of  anything you can easily get by  performing just a single set of an  exercise and still have it be highly  valuable  now i know that some people are already  thinking studies suggest that performing  three sets of an exercise is superior to  performing one or two that way you get  more volume but not enough to cause  excessive fatigue but here's the problem  with studies they tend to look at black  and white scenarios three sets versus  two sets with all else being equal they  don't necessarily look at the way that  we can mitigate against the drawbacks of  one method or another they don't always  consider that there are third options  out there  the reason you don't need three sets is  that you can get the same results by  carefully selecting exercises that offer  similar benefits by choosing exercises  that have overlap say for example rather  than performing three sets of bicep  curls you could instead perform one set  of bicep curls one set of bodyweight  curls and one set of hands only rope  climbs each of these will slightly alter  the angle of resistance and the position  of your hand and grip however they're  similar enough that they will still  offer compounding benefits  this way you're also strengthening a  wider number of movement patterns you'll  be getting additional benefits such as  grip strength and core strength and  you'll be making your workouts far more  interesting  there are still arguments for performing  the precise same movement if your only  interest is in growth and if you want to  truly isolate and fatigue a specific  muscle group likewise if you're trying  to learn a specific technique then  there's not much better than rote  repetition but for most of us looking to  just look and feel a little bit stronger  while also becoming more functional and  more mobile one set of three similar  exercises is perfect it's superior even  or if you want to sit on the fence then  you can perform two sets of exercises  instead and that's pretty much what i do  most of the time  and it's not just the movement itself  that you can vary it's also the  difficulty the tempo and the structure  for example even when i do do multiple  sets of a single exercise i'll often mix  it up with a drop set or i'll include an  isometric hold at the end perhaps one  set is fast and heavy the other is slow  and light there's lots of ways to mix  things up of those three bicep exercise  examples i just gave the rope climb is  probably standing out as potentially the  most exciting for most people but 